module gunbc.credential_argv_exposure

import std.types { List, NonEmptyStr, String }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }

// A CREDENTIAL IN ARGV IS READ BY EVERYONE ON THE HOST, AND ON THIS FLEET THAT SENTENCE IS MEASURED
// RATHER THAN ASSUMED. The three facts that decide it were taken on srv1 on 2026-08-25: /proc is
// mounted with no hidepid, /proc/PID/cmdline is mode 0444, and the CI runner slots are ordinary host
// systemd services -- actions-runner@srv1-01 under system.slice, User=root dropping to ghrunner via
// setpriv -- running in the HOST pid namespace rather than a container. So the audience for an argv
// word is not "other processes owned by the same user"; it is any job payload executing on a runner
// slot, in a repository whose workflows a pull request can influence.
//
// That is why this class is recorded as an incident carrier rather than as hygiene. The usual reason
// argv exposure gets deprioritized is an implicit assumption that the reader must already be
// privileged. Here the reader is unprivileged and arrives through the front door.
data credential_argv_exposure_measured_surface_note: String = "srv1, 2026-08-25: /proc mounted rw,nosuid,nodev,noexec,relatime with NO hidepid; /proc/PID/cmdline mode 0444; actions-runner@srv1-01 is a host systemd service in the host pid namespace, not containerized. A host-level ps enumerated 22 session-container tmux processes whose argv carried live forwarded credentials. Measurement performed by session warm-tern-755 with fleet SSH; this module records the reading, it does not re-derive it."

// THE ROOT CAUSE OF THE REPORTED INCIDENT IS NOT IN THIS REPOSITORY, and saying so precisely is the
// point of this row. The observation that opened the incident -- GITHUB_TOKEN and BUILDBUDDY_API_KEY
// visible in ps -- comes from the session container entrypoint, which forwards a roster of names into
// a tmux invocation as `-e NAME=VALUE` arguments. Those become the argv of the long-lived tmux server
// and stay there for the life of the session. That file is baked into the session-dashboard container
// image; no checkout of it exists in this repository, so it cannot be repaired from here and this
// module does not pretend to have repaired it.
//
// The fix was established by execution rather than by reasoning, and is recorded here so the owning
// repository does not have to re-derive it: exporting the variables in the parent shell and dropping
// the `-e` arguments entirely leaves the value absent from the tmux server argv while the pane still
// receives it, because the server captures its own environment as the global environment at start.
// A planted canary is present in the argv under the `-e` form and absent under the export form, with
// the pane reading the correct value in both -- so the detector is discriminating rather than merely
// quiet. `tmux setenv` is NOT the fix: it relocates the value from a server argv to a client argv.
data credential_argv_exposure_out_of_repo_root_cause_note: String = "The reported instance lives in the session container entrypoint (session-dashboard infrastructure), which projects a forwarded credential roster into `tmux new-session -e NAME=VALUE` arguments. Out of scope for this repository by ownership, not by severity. Verified repair shape: export in the parent shell, drop -e; the tmux server inherits its environment as the global environment and panes inherit it, with the value absent from every argv. Routing to the owning repository is an operator decision recorded on the incident, not a change this module can make."

type CredentialArgvInstance {
  subject: NonEmptyStr
  mechanism: NonEmptyStr
  disposition: NonEmptyStr
}

// THE THREE INSTANCES THIS REPOSITORY OWNS, and they are listed together because the incident's
// value is the CLASS rather than the one process someone happened to run ps against. Each was found
// by asking the same question of our own tree that the reporter asked of the host: which credential
// becomes a command-line word before exec.
data redfish_basic_auth_instance: CredentialArgvInstance = CredentialArgvInstance {
  subject: "extdeps.bmc.http GetSystem / GetPower / GetThermal" as NonEmptyStr,
  mechanism: "declared `password: Secret` interpolated into the argv token `-u \{username}:\{password}`; bmc_onboard called GetSystem with the freshly ROTATED credential, so the new password entered argv at the moment the old one was retired" as NonEmptyStr,
  disposition: "REPAIRED. The three argv-borne arms are deleted at the root rather than deprecated, and every caller authenticates through a 0600 netrc. `Secret` no longer appears anywhere in that module." as NonEmptyStr
}

data secret_manager_bearer_instance: CredentialArgvInstance = CredentialArgvInstance {
  subject: "gunbc.ci_spec fleet-key and spark-grant preludes" as NonEmptyStr,
  mechanism: "`curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer $WIF_ACCESS_TOKEN\"` -- the shell expands before exec, so the GCP access token is a curl argv word on the runner, readable by exactly the audience measured above" as NonEmptyStr,
  disposition: "REPAIRED. The header is written to a 0600 file by the `printf` BUILTIN, which forks no process and so creates no second command line, and curl reads it with `-H @FILE`." as NonEmptyStr
}

// THE UNREPAIRED ONE IS NAMED HERE RATHER THAN LEFT FOR REDISCOVERY, because a partial repair that
// is silent about its remainder is how a class gets marked closed while an instance survives. This
// one is the worst of the three by blast radius and the most expensive to fix, which is exactly the
// combination that gets quietly dropped from a security change.
//
// It is also the only one whose repair is NOT the same move as the other two. websocat has no
// `@file` header form, and the argv is handed to `systemd-run --unit`, which performs no shell
// expansion -- that is precisely why an earlier change replaced a `$BMCWEB_SESSION_TOKEN` reference
// with the materialized value, and it was right about the mechanism. The consequence is that the
// token reaches the transient unit's recorded ExecStart and the journal, not merely the process
// table. The repair shape is a systemd EnvironmentFile at 0600 with the argv carrying an unexpanded
// reference resolved by an `sh -c` wrapper, which moves the value into /proc/PID/environ (mode 0400,
// owner-only) rather than cmdline (0444, world-readable).
//
// TWO WITNESSES CURRENTLY ASSERT THE TOKEN IS IN THE ARGV and would have to be inverted with it, so
// this is not a one-line change dressed up as a large one: a probe written to prove the value was
// MATERIALIZED rather than left as a dead shell reference has, as a side effect, frozen the argv
// placement as a contract. That is worth recording on its own -- an assertion can pin the defect it
// was never asked about.
data bmcweb_session_token_instance: CredentialArgvInstance = CredentialArgvInstance {
  subject: "extdeps.bmc.webui.nbd_proxy_serve nbd_proxy_serve_websocat_argv" as NonEmptyStr,
  mechanism: "the live BMCWEB session token is concatenated into a `--header=` argv word and handed to systemd_run RunTransient, whose own argv is `systemd-run --unit=\{unit} --collect -- \{command_argv}`. So the token occupies THREE positions, not one: the systemd-run process's own argv while it runs, the websocat process's argv for the whole lifetime of the served ISO, and systemd's recorded ExecStart for the transient unit -- which is what reaches the journal and `systemctl show`. The middle one is long-lived and is the identical world-readable cmdline surface as the incident that opened this carrier" as NonEmptyStr,
  disposition: "NOT REPAIRED in this change, and deliberately so. websocat has no @file header form and systemd-run does not expand shell references, so the repair is a systemd EnvironmentFile at 0600 plus an sh -c wrapper -- a different mechanism from the other two instances, on the srv3 OS-install path, requiring two witnesses that currently ASSERT the token's presence in argv to be inverted. Rushing it inside a security change risks breaking an install path to close a leak that is already declared." as NonEmptyStr
}

// THE JOURNAL HALF IS MEASURED, AND THE ANSWER IS "UNEXERCISED", WHICH IS NOT "CLEAN". Run on all
// four fleet hosts on 2026-08-25 against the derivable unit glob `nbd-proxy-*-ws`
// (nbd_proxy_serve_websocat_unit_name builds `nbd-proxy-<lease_key>-ws`): units=0, journal_lines=0,
// grep_journal=0, execstart_now=0, live websocat processes=0, on every host.
//
// BOTH CONTROLS ARE ZERO, so the greps distinguish NOTHING. No such unit has ever existed and the
// journal holds no lines for that selection, which means the srv3 OS-install route has not run on
// this fleet. The correct reading is that the leak is LATENT rather than absent: the code path is
// real and would expose the token on all three surfaces the moment it runs. A zero measured against
// no history is not evidence of safety, and recording it as one would be the same non-result this
// lane has now produced repeatedly.
//
// THE DETECTOR RULE THAT PRODUCED THREE FALSE POSITIVES BEFORE THIS READING WAS TRUSTED, kept because
// none of the three was caught by reading the code and all three had one shape. A first pass reported
// live token-bearing processes on all four hosts; every hit was `grep` MATCHING ITS OWN COMMAND LINE,
// since the search pattern is itself an argv word that ps can see. Excluding the searcher's own
// process returns zero. The same artifact inflated an unrelated orphan census, and the same artifact
// made this module's own curl repair read as a non-repair until the probe was narrowed to the subject
// process's own cmdline.
//
// So: any ps- or /proc-based detector must exclude its own process, and the receipt for a ZERO must
// be a PLANTED POSITIVE rather than the absence of hits. That is not a nicety here -- it is the same
// defect this whole carrier is about, turned on the instrument: a value in a command line read by
// something that had no business reading it, which in that case was the measurement itself.
data bmcweb_token_journal_measurement_note: String = "MEASURED 2026-08-25, all four fleet hosts, unit glob nbd-proxy-*-ws: units=0 journal_lines=0 grep_journal=0 execstart_now=0 live_websocat=0. BOTH CONTROLS ZERO, so this is UNEXERCISED PATH, not clean -- the srv3 install route has not run on this fleet and the leak is LATENT, not absent. Queries: `systemctl list-units --all 'nbd-proxy-*'` and `journalctl -u 'nbd-proxy-*-ws' --output=cat | wc -l` as the controls; `grep -c -F 'Authorization: Token'` over the same journal selection and over `systemctl show 'nbd-proxy-*-ws' -p ExecStart` as the measurements. DETECTOR RULE, from three false positives in one day: a ps/proc detector MUST exclude its own process (the search pattern is an argv word ps can see), and a zero is only meaningful against a planted positive."

// TWO AXES, AND A READER WHO COLLAPSES THEM WILL MIS-RANK THIS. Instance 3 is the most severe by
// SURFACE COUNT AND LIFETIME, and it is also the only one NOT CURRENTLY FIRING -- measured above as
// unexercised on all four hosts. Instances 1 and 2 were LIVE and are closed; instance 3 is LATENT and
// open. Both facts are needed: severity without liveness over-ranks it against work that is leaking
// today, and liveness without severity under-ranks a defect that exposes a credential on three
// surfaces the moment the install path runs.
//
// The unexercised finding also STRENGTHENS the decision to declare rather than repair. Nothing is
// leaking right now, so no emergency forces a risky change to the srv3 install path under time
// pressure, and the repair can be done properly -- with the two witnesses that currently assert the
// token's presence inverted alongside it, rather than bolted on afterwards.
data credential_argv_open_instance_is_the_most_severe_note: String = "RANKING ON TWO AXES, both required. SEVERITY: the open instance is the worst of the three -- argv PLUS a durable record. LIVENESS: it is the only one NOT currently firing (measured unexercised on all four hosts), while instances 1 and 2 were live and are now closed. Severity alone over-ranks it against work leaking today; liveness alone under-ranks a three-surface exposure that fires the moment the install path runs. Latency is also why declaring rather than repairing was right: no emergency forces a rushed change to the srv3 install path, so the repair can land properly with its two inverted witnesses. Original note follows -- RANKING, stated because the intuitive order is backwards: the two REPAIRED instances were argv-only on short-lived processes and are closed; the one still OPEN (nbd_proxy_serve) is argv PLUS a durable record across three surfaces -- systemd-run's transient argv, websocat's argv for the lifetime of the served ISO, and the recorded ExecStart which persists in the journal and systemctl show after the process exits. Revoking the token stops it working without removing it from the record. This instance outranks the binder trigger scoped below for whoever picks up next work."

data credential_argv_exposure_instances: List<CredentialArgvInstance> = [
  redfish_basic_auth_instance,
  secret_manager_bearer_instance,
  bmcweb_session_token_instance,
]

// THE RUNG, STATED AT THE HONEST GRAIN. What landed is two repairs and one declaration, not a wall.
// Nothing in this repository refuses the next author who interpolates a credential into a command
// string, and the reason is structural rather than an oversight: v2.std.operation_argv's binding wall
// already refuses undeclared names and a non-literal executable position, but ShellTransportOperationRow
// carries `declared_inputs` as a List<String> -- NAMES WITHOUT TYPES -- so the binder cannot see that
// an input is `Secret` and could not refuse it if it wanted to. The hand-authored shell in ci_spec is
// further out still: it is not declared operation argv at all, so no binder sees it under any change.
//
// So the class sits at MITIGATABLE and its next-rung trigger is a specific missing capability, not a
// resolution to be careful: the declared input's TYPE reaching ShellTransportOperationRow, at which
// point `Secret` bound into an argv token becomes a typed refusal beside UndeclaredInputBound and the
// bmc instance above becomes unwritable rather than merely rewritten. Naming that trigger is what
// separates "cannot climb further" from "can climb after one grounding"; this is the second.
data credential_argv_exposure_rung_note: String = "RUNG: mitigatable. Two instances repaired by construction at their own call sites, one declared unrepaired with its mechanism and repair shape. NOT mechanically preventable: v2.std.operation_argv refuses undeclared names and a non-literal argv[0], but ShellTransportOperationRow carries declared_inputs as names without types, so a Secret-typed input bound into an argv token is invisible to the binder. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: carry the declared input TYPE on that row, then refuse Secret-into-argv-token as a typed cause alongside UndeclaredInputBound. Until that lands, this class is held by review diligence, which is strictly weaker and is recorded as such rather than counted as coverage."

// THE NEXT ITEM, SCOPED HERE AND DELIBERATELY NOT STARTED IN THIS CHANGE. Everything above is two
// repairs and a declaration -- the class is held by review diligence, and review diligence is what
// failed for however long the three instances stood. The construction answer is one specific missing
// fact, and naming its shape is what makes it a queued item rather than an aspiration.
//
// WHAT IS MISSING: v2.std.operation_argv's ShellTransportOperationRow carries `declared_inputs` as a
// List<String>. Names, not types. The binder therefore knows THAT an operation declares `password`
// and cannot know that `password` was declared `Secret`, so the refusal it would need to raise is not
// merely unwritten -- it is unexpressible against the row it would have to read.
//
// WHAT IT WOULD BUY: with the declared type on the row, `Secret` bound into an argv token becomes a
// typed cause beside UndeclaredInputBound, and instance 1 above stops being a thing an author
// rewrites and becomes a thing an author cannot write. That is the difference between this carrier
// being a record of three repairs and being the last time this class is found by reading.
//
// WHAT IT DOES NOT REACH, stated so the scope is not overclaimed when someone picks it up: the
// ci_spec instance is hand-authored shell text rather than declared operation argv, so no binder sees
// it under any version of this change. That half stays at review diligence plus the emission witness,
// and closing it would need shell command construction to become a modeled surface -- a much larger
// question that this trigger neither answers nor depends on.
data credential_argv_next_item_scope_note: String = "NEXT ITEM (scoped, not started): carry the declared input TYPE on v2.std.operation_argv ShellTransportOperationRow, which today holds declared_inputs as names only. That single fact is what makes a Secret-into-argv-token refusal expressible, turning the extdeps.bmc.http class from rewritten into unwritable. Explicitly out of its reach: gunbc.ci_spec's hand-authored shell is not declared operation argv and no binder change reaches it; that half stays at review diligence plus test.claim.credential_argv_exposure_witness until shell command construction is itself modeled."

data credential_argv_binder_trigger_ref: DeclarationRef = DeclarationRef {
  module_path: "v2.std.operation_argv",
  decl_name: "ShellTransportOperationRow",
  field: WholeDeclaration
}
